    "retirement_checks": 0,
    "note": "retirement_checks stays 0 because this scenario never calls DocStore.requestRetirement (nothing to retire for a no-consumer DB), so the owner-turn serviceRetirement wiring correctly never fires; background_turns/maintenance_leases are unchanged from before this round (the design doc's unreached 0/0/0 end state needs WS2's group-commit protocol and WS5's owner-queued retirement fully landed, not just this round's wiring -- see the capi-contract-test's own code comment for the full explanation)."
  },
  "known_limitations": [
    "This harness opens Lite directly through capi.openLiteHandleWithRuntime with a .manual BackendRuntime, bypassing capi/lite_connection.zig's Owner/connection-pool layer entirely. WS7 round 3's owner-turn retirement wiring (Owner.maintenanceTurn -> DocStore.serviceRetirement) and the allocator-certificate size threshold (Owner.notifyMutation) therefore cannot show up in this harness's numbers at all -- see capi-contract-test's 'no-consumer path 1037 real primary writes' test for a measurement that does exercise the Owner layer.",
    "primary_only's remaining background work (547 commits, unchanged from the pre-round-3 baseline; sync count did drop 12.82 -> 8.546875 per update, from other merged work on this integration branch, not this round's changes) is storage/lite/docstore.zig's own self-driven Store.maintenanceLoop performing allocator page reclamation independent of any Owner or consumer -- the 'below-threshold per-write ledger load' / reclamation ride-along work this round did not reach (see the accompanying report). This is the precise, concrete remaining gap toward the design doc's 0-background-commits primary-only target measured through this specific harness.",
    "indexed and indexed_8_writers dropped substantially too (calling-thread syncs/update 66.844 -> 32.5625), but this reflects other workstreams (WS1 native commit protocol, etc.) already merged into perf/lwa-integration ahead of this round, not anything this round changed."
  ],
